  body: "# Benefit disqualification in the captured custody guidance\n\nDisqualification from receiving
    benefit is distinct from the fact of imprisonment and from the initial suspension of payment. This
    profile covers only the captured imprisonment ground, not every ground of benefit disqualification.
    DMG 12003 describes the payability effect for its listed benefits; ESA(Cont) 53258–53260 supplies
    a separate more-than-six-week LCW qualification and no-penalty exception. The term alone does not
    establish a benefit, a ground, a current regime or an entitlement outcome.\n\n## Scope and limitations\n\
    \nFrozen DMG research: listed legacy JSA(Cont), JSA(IB), IS, SPC and ESA(Cont)/ESA(IR) imprisonment
    guidance captured on 15 September 2026. This is an explanation of the captured text, not a determination
    of contemporary individual eligibility.\n\nIndependent experimental research, not an official DWP
    document, benefits advice, a calculation or an individual entitlement decision. All authored meanings
    and dependencies require specialist review.\n\nThe broad labels JSA and ESA do not establish a claimant’s
    regime. This profile explains the listed DMG legacy regimes; New Style JSA/ESA, UC and PIP are routed
    to ADM by 12016. Those ADM bodies are not acquired. Ask for regime/date and fresh authoritative checks
    before applying guidance to a person.\n\nThe payability statement at 12003 is scoped by the list in
    12002. It cannot replace benefit-specific rules or establish that entitlement always survives imprisonment.\n\
    \nMachine-extracted whole pages retain neighbouring text, partial unrelated paragraphs, footnotes,
    historical examples and possible reading-order errors. Use the stated locators; page presence is not
    full legal interpretation or PDF visual review.\n\nCapture date is not publication, revision or commencement.
    Old amendment footers and example dates remain literal source text. The currently linked catalogue
    is not current-law assurance; legislation and case law have not been consolidated or reconciled.\n\
    \nThe frozen PDFs still refer to Memo 07/20. Official catalogue history says it was removed because
    the regulations ceased to have effect from 31 August 2021. The memo body is absent; do not revive
    that temporary exception.\n\n12016’s ADM M1 reference conflicts with the frozen catalogue title marking
    M1 spare. S1/U6 are identified routes, not acquired evidence. Internal Chapter 41 references to 53112
    and 53071 and the printed Chapter 53 number 531261 remain unresolved version/typographic observations.\n\
    \nPrisoner definitions differ by benefit: do not transfer the general criminal/civil distinction indiscriminately.
    Remand, bail hostel, sentence, release, hospital legislation, fine default, overseas detention, time
    limits and partner circumstances require the relevant branch. Housing-cost passages do not establish
    a current housing award."
